Chapter 8: Recommendations and Future Research

8.1 Introduction

This chapter translates the analytical findings of the thesis into two distinct streams: strategic recommendations for the Chinese lunar architecture, and directions for future academic research. The recommendations are not operational directives; they are theoretically grounded policy insights derived from the ASH framework, the SCI domain scores, and the binary-fork decision architecture. As with the entire thesis, no privileged information is used, and no physical tests were conducted. The proposals represent the logical endpoint of a net assessment conducted entirely from open-source data.

8.2 Strategic Recommendations for China’s Lunar Architecture

The following proposals are organised around the highest-leverage interventions that the SCI and binary-fork analysis identify. Each is supported by the evidence presented in earlier chapters.

8.2.1 Decouple the Crewed Landing Schedule from Settlement Power Requirements

The Long March-10, Mengzhou, and Lanyue stack has achieved integration milestones that make a sub-2030 crewed landing highly credible. That success creates political pressure to declare a permanent base upon landing. The Elena-AM reactor, at 68 kWe, is insufficient for settlement operations and its timeline is hostage to Russian execution risk. The recommendation is explicit: lock the crewed landing for 2029–2030, but gate reactor deployment to demonstrated ISRU pilot-plant power demand, not to a calendar year. A landing proves logistics; it does not prove habitability. The distinction must be protected from political conflation.

8.2.2 Institutionalise Chang’e-7 as a Formal Binary-Fork Decision Gate

The thesis provides the quantitative thresholds, the independent review protocol, and the branching architecture required to make Chang’e-7 the irreversible pivot point of the ILRS. The recommendation is that CNSA publish explicit go/no-go criteria before launch, commission a red-team/blue-team analysis with external validation, and commit to an architecture redesign if the “No-Go” conditions are met. Transparency in this decision process would signal programme maturity to potential international partners and would defend against the sunk-cost fallacy that awaits any ambiguous data set.

8.2.3 Scope Chang’e-8 as a TRL-6 Failure-Mode Diagnostic, Not a Showcase

Chang’e-8 should be instrumented to measure dust accumulation, thermal cycling degradation, seal abrasion, and extraction efficiency decay. Its success is not a claim of sustainable presence but a detailed failure-mode catalogue that informs TRL-7 development. Specific demonstration targets—microwave water extraction at 100 grams per hour continuous for seven days, regolith 3D-printing of a structural brick exceeding 50 MPa—should be formally required and publicly reported.

8.2.4 Initiate a Parallel China-Led 500 kWe-Class Surface Reactor Programme

The Russia-China Elena-AM track delivers 68 kWe on a schedule with high uncertainty. Settlement-class ISRU and life support require 250–500 kWe. A parallel indigenous small modular reactor programme, with a target of TRL-4 by 2030, TRL-6 by 2035, and autonomous deployment by 2038–2042, is the single most important hedge against the programme’s largest schedule risk. The “555 Project” could be used to source international thermal and radiation expertise while retaining system integration under Chinese control.

8.2.5 Standardise Queqiao Constellation ICDs Early to Lower Coalition Barriers

The ILRS coalition is currently asymmetric: political signatories outnumber engineering contributors. Publishing interface control documents, power and data budgets, and radiation specifications by 2027 would lower the barrier for partners to contribute flight hardware. If partners cannot or will not contribute at engineering scale, the programme should plan for a solo build rather than maintain a coalition of convenience that obscures the true capability baseline.

8.2.6 Fund a Lunar Dust Mitigation Grand Challenge Separate from ECLSS

Dust is a system-level threat that attacks seals, bearings, optics, thermal control surfaces, and spacesuits, and it interacts lethally with the 14-day thermal cycle. A dedicated three-year terrestrial programme exposing candidate materials and subsystems to lunar dust simulant, thermal cycling, UV radiation, and vacuum is a precondition for credible ECLSS and surface mobility development. The Tiangong artificial photosynthesis breakthrough, while significant, remains a LEO demonstration in a dust-free, crew-tended environment.

8.2.7 Exploit the Artemis Restructuring Tactically, Without Mirroring Its Structural Errors

The cancellation of the Lunar Gateway and the Artemis programme’s schedule shift open a near-term landing-sequence window. The recommendation is to use that window to consolidate reliability rather than to rush beyond technology readiness. Freezing the LM-10/Mengzhou/Lanyue configuration after the uncrewed flight and resisting scope creep would contrast positively with the churn that has characterised Artemis. Muscle memory—repeated, reliable flights—matters more than a marginal acceleration of the landing date.

8.2.8 Accept That a Negative Chang’e-7 Result is Architecture-Driving Data, Not a Public Relations Problem

A finding of no accessible ice does not mean the ILRS fails; it means the ILRS is re-baselined as an Earth-supplied, solar-powered sortie base. The architecture and associated public narrative must be redesigned in advance for this contingency. Announcing a “permanent base by 2035” while depending on Earth consumables would repeat the milestone-centric logic the ASH critiques and would undermine credibility when logistics costs become apparent.

8.2.9 Protect LM-10 Production and Reusability as the Hidden Logistics Bottleneck

Launch cadence is the rate-limiting factor for both the ISRU-led and the Earth-supplied paths. The February 2026 booster recovery is promising but not yet an operational system. If stage recovery rates fall below 50 percent, the required new-core production doubles, with attendant schedule penalties. A sensitivity analysis on reusability performance should inform logistics planning, and a minimum cadence of two launches per year during construction (four during operations) must be maintained.

8.2.10 Treat the Term “Permanent Base” as an Engineering Threshold, Not a Political Declaration

The ASH defines permanence by the closure of power, ISRU, and ECLSS loops, not by the presence of humans on the surface. The recommendation is that no official ILRS document use the term “permanent base” until the TRL-8 gates identified in Chapter 5 are satisfied. A more accurate descriptor for the 2030–2040 period is “tended outpost,” which accurately reflects dependence on Earth and the hibernation constraints of solar-only power.

8.3 Future Research Directions

The thesis opens several avenues for further academic investigation. These are presented as a structured research agenda.

8.3.1 Sensitivity Analysis and Weighting Calibration

The SCI domain weights were derived from a physics-based logic, but they are subject to sensitivity. Future work should perform a formal sensitivity analysis, systematically varying domain weights and proxy indicator thresholds to test the robustness of the composite score and the resulting scenario probability bands. An expert elicitation process—surveying lunar scientists, systems engineers, and space policy analysts—could calibrate the weights against a broader evidence base.

8.3.2 Extension to Other Spacefaring Actors

The ASH–SCI framework is designed to be actor-agnostic. Future studies could apply the same methodology to the United States’ Artemis programme, India’s lunar ambitions, or a hypothetical commercial settlement venture. A comparative SCI matrix would enable a structured strategic assessment of the relative maturity of competing lunar settlement efforts.

8.3.3 Dynamic Modelling of ISRU and Settlement Scaling

The transition from grams-per-minute laboratory extraction to tonnes-per-year industrial production is a complex, non-linear process. Future research could develop a dynamic systems model that incorporates learning curves, failure-mode probabilities, and mass-flow logistics to generate probabilistic distributions for settlement timelines, replacing the qualitative scenarios of this thesis with quantitative uncertainty ranges.

8.3.4 Incorporation of Lunar Geological Uncertainty

The Chang’e-7 binary fork depends on a single mission’s data. Lunar ice distribution is spatially heterogeneous; a PSR that appears ISRU-viable from one hopper probe may be unrepresentative of the broader crater floor. Future work could integrate orbital neutron spectrometry, thermal modelling, and geological analogues to produce prior probability maps of ISRU viability before in-situ data arrive, refining the Bayesian decision gate.

8.3.5 Open-Source Intelligence Methodology Refinement

The thesis contributes one OSINT-based capability index, but the methodology can be deepened. Machine learning tools for parsing multilingual technical publications, automated change-detection on satellite imagery of launch sites, and natural language processing of state media statements could augment the data collection and validation protocol. Research into the reliability and bias patterns of different open-source outlets would strengthen the epistemic foundations of OSINT-based space analysis.

8.3.6 Real-Time Validation Against Programme Decisions

The Chang’e-7 binary-fork architecture includes specific go/no-go criteria and architecture commitments. A longitudinal study could track, over the 2027–2035 period, whether China’s actual programme decisions align with or diverge from the normative architecture proposed here. Such a study would provide a rare empirical test of decision-gate theory in a real-world, high-stakes aerospace programme.

8.3.7 Extension to Mars Settlement Planning

The ASH logic—that accumulation of logistics, robotics, power, and life-support deposits, rather than a single landing, determines settlement viability—applies with even greater force to Mars, where the logistical distance is orders of magnitude greater. A Mars-specific SCI could be developed, incorporating the additional challenges of transit duration, atmospheric ISRU, and communication latency, to produce a comparative settlement maturity assessment for the next frontier.

8.4 Closing Reflection

This thesis has argued that the Moon yields on its own schedule, and that the honest confrontation of that schedule—through open-source data, physical constraints, and pre-committed decision gates—is the hallmark of a strategically mature space programme. The recommendations and research directions offered here are an invitation to deepen that confrontation, both in the policy chambers where architecture decisions are made and in the academic forums where the tools for making them are refined. The 21st-century lunar race is not a sprint to a flag; it is a multi-decade accumulation of engineering deposits whose final shape will be determined not by any single landing, but by the physics that governs the long lunar night, the dusty regolith, and the fragile, irreplaceable human breath.


