Appendix C: Chang’e-7 Binary-Fork Decision Architecture — Thresholds and Logic

C.1 ISRU Viability Energy Model

The go/no-go thresholds are derived from a break-even analysis of the energy cost of extracting water from regolith versus shipping water from Earth.

Assumptions:

· Specific energy of microwave extraction: 5–15 kWh/kg H₂O (based on lab data, 1.57 g/min at bench scale).
· Embodied energy of Earth-shipped water: sum of launch, trans-lunar injection, landing propellant, and tankage. LM-10 delivers ~27 t to TLI; water is ~10% of payload mass after accounting for containment. Estimated effective energy cost ~30–50 kWh/kg delivered to surface.
· Thus, if ice concentration yields an extraction energy below ~30 kWh/kg, ISRU is favourable.

The model yields: >3 wt% ice at <1 m depth is clearly favourable; <1 wt% clearly unfavourable. Intermediate region (1–3 wt%) is marginal, conservatively assigned to No-Go.

C.2 Go/No-Go Criteria Table

Parameter Go Threshold No-Go Trigger
Water ice concentration ≥3 wt% average in top 1 m <1 wt% in all sampled PSRs
Overburden depth ≤1 m dry regolith 2 m overburden in all sampled PSRs
Terrain slope <15° within 10 km radius 25° slopes dominate, inaccessible
Spatial redundancy ≥3 separate PSRs meeting all Go criteria Fewer than 2 PSRs with confirmed ice at any grade

C.3 Decision Phase Flow

· Phase 0 (Pre-launch, Aug 2026): Publish criteria; verify instruments at TRL-6; ensure dual-string relay.
· Phase 1 (Q4 2026–Q1 2027): 90-day raw data embargo; Red Team/Blue Team analysis; external CAS + 2 non-Chinese labs audit; Go requires >0.8 posterior probability.
· Phase 1B (Q2 2027, if No-Go): Re-baseline to Earth-supplied sortie base; solar + fuel cells, 14-day hibernation; LM-10 at 4×/year; ECLSS closure relaxed to >85%; no “permanent base” label.
· Phase 2 (2027–2028, if Go): Freeze Chang’e-8 scope (ISRU extraction pilot, regolith 3D printing); split nuclear path (Track A Elena-AM, Track B China-led 500 kWe); lock LM-10 cadence at 2/year construction, 4/year ops.
· Phase 3 (2028–2048): TRL-8 gates (see Appendix D).

